{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "esnext"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "jsx": "preserve",
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "next"
      }
    ],
    "paths": {
{{- if .Workspaces}}
      "@/components/ui/*": ["../../packages/ui-web/src/components/ui/*"],
{{- end}}
      "@connectrpc/connect": ["./node_modules/@connectrpc/connect"],
      "@bufbuild/protobuf": ["./node_modules/@bufbuild/protobuf"],
      "@opentelemetry/api": ["./node_modules/@opentelemetry/api"],
      "@tanstack/react-query": ["./node_modules/@tanstack/react-query"],
      "@tanstack/query-core": ["./node_modules/@tanstack/query-core"],
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    }
  },
  "// peer dedupe note": "The @connectrpc/@bufbuild/@opentelemetry/@tanstack entries in paths pin @reliantlabs/forge-web-runtime's peerDependencies to THIS app's copy, for the same reason next.config.ts redirects them in the webpack resolver. @tanstack/react-query is listed because the generated hooks EXPOSE its types (UseQueryOptions, ThrowOnError, Query) in their own signatures, so two copies fail with 'Property #private in type Query refers to a different member'; query-core is listed with it because react-query re-exports those types FROM it, and pinning only the wrapper leaves the underlying copy split. A dev build of forge bridges the runtime with a `file:` specifier, which npm materialises as a symlink into the forge checkout; that checkout carries its own node_modules, so the runtime's bare imports resolve there while this app's resolve here. The bundler fix in next.config.ts does not help `tsc --noEmit`, which does not run webpack: typecheck saw two nominally-distinct copies of the same package and failed with TS2322 'Type Transport is not assignable to type Transport' in src/lib/mock-transport_gen.ts. Keep the list in step with next.config.ts and the vite-spa config. This note sits at the top level, NOT inside compilerOptions, because tsc validates that object and rejects an unknown key there with TS5023.",
  "include": ["next-env.d.ts", "**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx"],
  "// exclude note": "Exclude the Next build dirs wholesale (not just subpaths). Next writes per-route type stubs under .next{,-prod}/types that import the route module by path; deleting a scaffolded route leaves a STALE stub whose import now dangles, so `tsc --noEmit` would spew TS2307 for a route that no longer exists. .next-prod is forge's production distDir (see next.config.ts). Excluding both wins even when `next dev`/`next build` re-adds .next/types/**/*.ts to `include` (TypeScript's exclude beats include).",
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".next", ".next-prod"]
}
